[2] Temporary effect timeouts:
Cursed bat: 5-20 turns.
uncursed bat: 40-100 turns
blessed bat: 100-500 turns
Timeouts replace one another rather than stack (ie if I get 50 turns
telepathy from one whack, then whack myself again and score 40 turns,
I end up with 40 turns, not 50 or 90) Temporary stat modifications
(both positive and negative) can be taken away with unihorn
application.

[3] Headgear. Most headgear is unaffected by the cluebat, and the
bat's effects generally ignore headgear. However:
- if the bat is negatively enchanted and the player is wearing a helm
of brilliance, a 'decrease int/wis' result will reduce the helm's
enchantment accordingly, and spare the player the stat decrease.
- if the bat is negatively enchanted and the player is wearing a helm
of telepathy, a "remove telepathy" result will convert the [oT into a
normal helm. If the player has intrinsic telepathy, they will keep
it.
- if the bat is negatively enchanted and the player is a wizard
wearing a cornuthaum at the time, a "remove clairvoyance" result will
convert the cornuthaum into a dunce hat. If the player has intrinsic
clairvoyance, they will keep it.
- There is also a 1% chance that *any* cluebat hit (regardless of bat
enchantment/BUC and in addition to any other result) on a worn helm of
opposite alignment will confuse and stun the player and convert him to
a random alignment.

[4] Yes, I realise this gives illiterate players the chance to bypass
spellbooks and learn new spells. It's a very very small chance,
however, and you have to club yourself silly to do it. If you *still*
think that throws balance, just remove this option and reassign that
1% wherever you like.
